{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA A records a fixed AI, 48 tasks and one score. Under B9, repeatability evidence is insufficient (0), not evidence of instability. Task and scoring descriptions inform B6 but do not establish validity under B10.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The passage does not establish whether repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "One reported score cannot demonstrate variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA A records one evaluation. B9 repeatability remains unrated for insufficient evidence (0); the explicit absence of repeats establishes that repeatability was not studied, without establishing instability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The record explicitly excludes repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "An absence of repeats supplies no findings demonstrating instability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA B9.3/B9.7, repeated evaluations provide limited evidence of score consistency for this AI and protocol. The two-point range meets the report's three-point rule; that rule is not an EFPA rating anchor, and broader precision remains unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The range is two percentage points, within the stated maximum of three."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA B9.3/B9.7, the repeated scores demonstrate inconsistency against the report's specified rule: their range is 31 points. This finding concerns this AI and protocol; it does not establish precision for other systems or conditions.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The repeated evaluations explicitly used matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The 31-point range exceeds the stated three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA B6 distinguishes documentation of topics and scoring from evidence of technical quality. B9 repeatability and B6.1/B7 answer quality cannot be established from this passage; missing findings do not demonstrate defective answers.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The supplied material does not establish a repeated-evaluation study."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "No evidence demonstrating answer mistakes is supplied."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_review": "The appendix contributes to EFPA B6.3.8 technical instructions and A5 supply information. It adds no B9 repeatability evidence or B6.1/B7 answer-quality findings; installation documentation cannot resolve those questions.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "Installation information does not establish repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results or examination of answers",
          "reason": "The appendix supplies no evidence demonstrating answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA B6.1.3 and B7 recognize independent editorial examination as development evidence. Reconciliation does not establish initial agreement or released-answer correctness. B9.6 would require agreement evidence if these judgments support operational scoring; applicability is otherwise unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release",
          "reason": "The passage describes developmental checking of both questions and answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "Examination and reconciliation do not establish that all released answers are correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA B6.1.3 records developmental examination, while B7 and B6.3.2 identify a demonstrated answer-key defect: twelve released answers are wrong. Initial editor agreement remains unknown; editorial review does not negate the documented errors.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release",
          "reason": "Both components underwent developmental examination."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "The released version explicitly contains incorrect answers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA B6.3.2 requires evidence that automated scoring is correct. Deterministic marking establishes consistency for identical text, not correctness or overall B9 precision. B9.6 is inapplicable to the described nonjudgmental software marking.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported",
          "reason": "An unreported comparison cannot establish that testing occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistent marks can still be incorrect; universal correctness is unsupported."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA B6.3.2 identifies a scoring check and two unresolved discrepancies against correct marks. Determinism does not resolve this demonstrated scoring problem. The 20 chosen responses do not establish coverage of all responses; B9.6 does not apply to nonjudgmental software marking.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly reports the comparison."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved",
          "reason": "The reported marks disagree with independently established correct marks, contradicting universal correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA B10 evaluates the stated interpretation: the counts support this evaluation's narrow comparison. Under B8.3.2.2, external norms are unnecessary for that sole raw-score comparison. Broader validity and B9 repeatability remain unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported counts directly answer the stated question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24",
          "reason": "A's reported count exceeds B's on the evaluated problems."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA B10 requires evidence supporting the intended interpretation across its claimed scope. These grid results establish a local comparison but do not establish general intelligence across subjects and settings. B8 norms and B9 precision for that broader use are also unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied",
          "reason": "The evidence does not justify the broad intended inference; it does not prove that inference false."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24",
          "reason": "A solved more of the evaluated grid problems under the reported conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA A3 and B6.1.3.1/B6.3.2 show alignment between scoring and the stated requirement. B10 supports this limited scoring interpretation; partial-credit omission is intentional. Scoring alignment alone does not establish precision, answer correctness or broader validity.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "The stated scoring requirement is to count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "The rule awards points exactly for the required complete-grid successes."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero credit for partial correctness follows the stated requirement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA A3 and B6.1.3.1/B6.3.2 reveal a direct conflict between implemented scoring and the required cell-level credit. Under B10, scores do not implement that stated interpretation. This is demonstrated misalignment, rather than merely missing evidence.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "The stated scoring requirement is to give a response credit for every correctly completed cell",
          "reason": "The all-or-nothing rule withholds the required credit for correct cells in partial outputs."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "including partially correct output grids",
          "reason": "The requirement explicitly includes credit that the zero-for-partial rule denies."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA A2.8 and B6.2.4 identify instruction wording as a changing administration condition. B9 identical-condition repeatability is not studied; B9.7/B10.3.1.6 can consider instruction sensitivity. The passage supplies neither effect sizes nor enough detail for a broader causal conclusion.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction",
          "reason": "Instructions changed, so the full evaluation conditions were not identical."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes while varying instructions and holding the listed other conditions fixed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA A2.8 and B9.3/B9.7 identify an identical-condition repeatability study. Actual score values and precision estimates are absent, so stability cannot be rated. Instruction sensitivity under B10.3.1.6 is not studied.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "The software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly reports score changes across matching evaluation conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied",
          "reason": "Instruction variation was explicitly excluded."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No instruction required clarification. The original EFPA model was retained: Part A describes each case; Part B sections 6–12 assess documentation, materials, norms, precision, validity, fair use and reports; Part C is limited to the supplied passages and EFPA 2025 text (CC BY-SA 4.0, https://www.efpa.eu/). Reinterpretations: test taker means AI, administration means evaluation protocol, and retesting means repeated AI evaluation. Human bodily prerequisites, participant debriefing and human demographic accommodations are inapplicable as literal AI-testing questions; language/interface requirements and fairness for relevant uses require separate evidence. Unmentioned factual fields, adaptation, supply arrangements, subgroup analyses and report properties remain unknown in each case. A result card is not automatically an interpretive digital report; report availability and the three-profile review requirement cannot be established. Ratings retain n/a and 0–4 meanings: 0 denotes insufficient information, not demonstrated inadequacy. Probe labels are separate from EFPA ratings. EFPA's restriction for critical 0/1 attributes limits warranted use without proving poor quality; broader or consequential uses lack support where critical evidence is missing. No aggregate benchmark-quality score or EFPA endorsement is implied."
}
